    <item>
      <title>Let&#39;s Encrypt wildcard certificates</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/letsencrypt-wildcard/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 12 Jan 2020 23:11:58 +0100</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/letsencrypt-wildcard/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;install-certbot&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Install certbot&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;heading-link&#34; href=&#34;#install-certbot&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;i class=&#34;fa-solid fa-link&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34; title=&#34;Link to heading&#34;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&#xA;    &lt;span class=&#34;sr-only&#34;&gt;Link to heading&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Certbot is the official command line tool to manage Let&amp;rsquo;s Encrypt certificates.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Since certbot is a python application, it is best to install it via python packet manager: pip.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;# pip install certbot&#xA;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;p&gt;In order to issue wildcard certificates you need to use DNS authentication method. Certbot has many DNS providers covered, you can list them with: &lt;code&gt;certbot plugins&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I will continue using OVH as DNS provider.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
